
  Also worth saying out loud: there are no tasks yet. Whatever trigger you pick, the task model has to grow the fields
  it needs (a due date, a status, a "watched" flag), so that's part of this work, not a prerequisite someone else does.

  [1mThe fork:[0m what event is worth interrupting the user for.

  - [1mTime-based (due soon / overdue)[0m — the one trigger that carries real information in a single-user app, because time
    passes without the user acting. Needs due dates on tasks and a timer while the page is open. Caveat: with no server
    and no service worker, nothing can reach the user when the tab is closed — a reminder for 3pm fires when they next
    have the page open, not at 3pm. That's an inherent ceiling of client-only, not a design flaw I can code around.
  - [1mAction feedback (toasts)[0m — "Task saved", "3 tasks deleted", with undo. Genuinely useful and cheap, but it's a toast
    component, not a notifications subsystem. Honest framing matters here: if this is what you want, the work is much
    smaller than "build a notifications system."
  - [1mBoth, over a shared surface[0m — one notification centre that renders time-based reminders and action feedback through
    the same queue and UI, with a "watched/starred" flag deciding which tasks generate reminders. More structure, and
    the extra cost is small if we build it once.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m the third, but only because the shared surface is cheap when built from scratch — if you build
  toasts now and add reminders later, you rewrite the plumbing. The substance is the time-based reminders; action
  feedback rides along nearly free. I'd skip anything speculative about future collaborators — no user model, no
  read/unread sync, no delivery preferences. Those are wrong to guess at and easy to add once a backend actually exists.
